    <mytitle|Where we are>

    \;

    At each lattice <math|N>, exactly (Lecture 4):

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>Y<rsub|q>>|<cell|=<around*|[|b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>*Z<rsub|q>|]>\<mathd\>q+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,>>|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>Z<rsub|q>>|<cell|=-<around*|[|b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>*Z<rsub|q>|]>\<mathd\>q+\<Xi\><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,>>>>
      </aligned>

      <space|2em>\<psi\>=Y+Z=<big|int><math-up|Cov><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W.
    </equation*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Inputs.>

    <\enumerate-Alpha>
      <item>Coercivity: <math|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>\<asymp\><around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>\<le\><around|(|1+\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>|)>/q>,
      <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>=C*<around|(|g<rsub|q>+g<rsub|q><rsup|2>|)>>.

      <item>Source: <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<le\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*q<rsup|-5/2>>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<le\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|4>*q<rsup|-5><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>>.

      <item>Coordinates: <math|K<rsub|q>*<around|(|Y<rsub|q>+\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>|)>+<frac|\<gamma\>|3>*Y<rsub|q><rsup|3>=P<rsub|q>>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<varrho\><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>\<le\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>>.
    </enumerate-Alpha>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Goal>

      <math|Z> small and <math|Y> rarely at large fields, <strong|uniformly
      in <math|N>>. Neither equation can be estimated alone.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|How FBSDE estimates usually work, and why not here>

    \;

    <with|font-series|bold|Usual> (Ma\UYong): Lipschitz coefficients, Itô on
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>> and Gronwall; close on a
    short interval or under monotonicity.

    <with|font-series|bold|Here:> infinite interval, coefficients not
    uniformly Lipschitz, the coupling is not small. Instead we use
    <strong|structure>:

    <\itemize>
      <item>the backward equation is <strong|linear in <math|Z>> given
      <math|Y>: a conditional representation with an explicit propagator;

      <item>the forward equation is, after subtracting the Gaussian part, a
      cubic <strong|elliptic> equation at each resolution: coercive,
      estimated <strong|pathwise>;

      <item>the two estimates are linked by two weighted energies with
      <strong|matched weights>.
    </itemize>

    At fixed <math|N,T> all moments are finite (finite dimensions): used only
    to justify absorptions. <small|<with|color|dark grey|Ma\UYong 1999>>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <with|font-size|1.6|font-series|bold|color|dark red|The energies>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture 5>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Subtracting the Gaussian part>

    \;

    Free field at resolution <math|q> driven by the <strong|same> innovation:

    <\equation*>
      X<rsub|q>=<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>C<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|q>>,<space|2em>K<rsub|q>*X<rsub|q>=W<rsub|q>+<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>X<rsub|s>\<mathd\>s.
    </equation*>

    Also <math|P<rsub|q>=W<rsub|q>+<big|int><around|(|Y+Z|)>>. With
    <math|V<rsub|q>=Y<rsub|q>+\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>-X<rsub|q>>
    and <math|F<rsup|Z><rsub|q>=<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>Z<rsub|s>\<mathd\>s>,
    the noise cancels:

    <\equation*>
      <frame|<space|0.27em>K<rsub|q>*V<rsub|q>+<frac|\<gamma\>|3>*Y<rsub|q><rsup|3>=<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>V<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s-<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>\<varrho\><rsub|s><around|(|Y<rsub|s>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s+F<rsup|Z><rsub|q>,<space|2em>Y<rsub|q>=V<rsub|q>+X<rsub|q>-\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>.<space|0.27em>>
    </equation*>

    <\itemize>
      <item>At each <math|q>: <math|<around|(|q-\<Delta\>|)>*V+<frac|\<gamma\>|3>*<around|(|V+X-\<varrho\>|)><rsup|3>=f>,
      a nonlinear Helmholtz equation; <math|X> is a shift inside the cube.

      <item>The memory has <strong|coefficient one>:
      <math|<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>V\<approx\>q*V>. It must be
      absorbed, not treated as forcing.

      <item>The backward equation enters only through
      <math|F<rsup|Z>=<big|int>Z>. Pathwise.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Thresholds and energies>

    \;

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|q>=<math-up|sgn><around|(|V<rsub|q>|)>*<around*|(|<around|\||V<rsub|q>|\|>-<tfrac|3|4>r<rsub|q>|)><rsub|+>,<space|2em>t<rsub|q>=Y<rsub|q>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|\||Y<rsub|q>|\|>\<gtr\>r<rsub|q>>,<space|2em>\<xi\><rsub|q>=X<rsub|q>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|\||X<rsub|q>|\|>\<gtr\>r<rsub|q>/32>.
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E<rsub|T>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|\<gamma\>><big|int>q<rsup|1/2><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q<space|2em><text|backward
        energy>>>|<row|<cell|J<rsub|T>>|<cell|=<big|int>q<rsup|-3/2>*<around*|[|q*<around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|q>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|4><rsup|4>+\<gamma\>|\<\|\|\>>*w<rsub|q>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|6><rsup|6>+\<gamma\>|\<\|\|\>>*t<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>|]>\<mathd\>q<space|2em><text|large-field
        action>>>|<row|<cell|G<rsub|T>>|<cell|=<big|int><around*|[|q<rsup|-1/2>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<xi\><rsub|q>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|4><rsup|4>+\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2>|\<\|\|\>>*\<xi\><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>|]>\<mathd\>q<space|2em><text|Gaussian
        tails>>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|quantity>|<cell|weight>|<cell|reason>>|<row|<cell|<math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>>|<cell|<math|q<rsup|1/2>>>|<cell|Hardy:
    <math|<big|int>q<rsup|-3/2><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsup|Z>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<le\>16*<big|int>q<rsup|1/2><around|\<\|\|\>|Z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<gamma\><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>>>|<cell|<math|q<rsup|-3/2>>>|<cell|<math|q<rsup|7/2><around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<le\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|3>\<cdot\>\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2><around|\<\|\|\>|t|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>>>>|<row|<cell|<math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>>|<cell|<math|q<rsup|7/2>>>|<cell|propagator
    <math|s/q>, <math|<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>>s<rsup|-3/2>=2*q<rsup|-1/2>>>>>>>

    Only <strong|thresholded> sixth powers: unthresholded,
    <math|\<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>\<asymp\>q<rsup|3/2>>
    for <math|2*q<rsub|0>\<le\>q\<lesssim\>N<rsup|2>>, so
    <math|<big|int>\<gamma\>*q<rsup|-3/2>*\<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>\<approx\>\<gamma\>*N<rsup|2>>
    is not uniform in <math|N>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Two Hardy inequalities>

    \;

    <\lemma*>
      For <math|f> on <math|<around|[|q<rsub|0>,\<infty\>|)>>, extended by
      zero to <math|<around|(|0,q<rsub|0>|)>>,

      <\equation*>
        <around*|\||q<rsup|-1>*<space|-0.17em><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|q>f|\|><rsub|L<rsup|4><around|(|q<rsup|-1/2>\<mathd\>q|)>>\<le\><frac|8|7><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|4><around|(|q<rsup|-1/2>\<mathd\>q|)>>,<space|2em><big|int>q<rsup|-3/2><around*|\||<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|q>f|\|><rsup|2>\<le\>16*<big|int>q<rsup|1/2><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>.
      </equation*>
    </lemma*>

    Proof: <math|q<rsup|-1>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|q>f=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>f<rsub|q*\<theta\>>\<mathd\>\<theta\>>,
    Minkowski; <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>\<theta\><rsup|-1/8>=<frac|8|7>>,
    <math|<around|(|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>\<theta\><rsup|-3/4>|)><rsup|2>=16>.

    <\myframed>
      <strong|The problem>

      <math|<frac|8|7>\<gtr\>1>: the mass term alone (coefficient one) does
      not absorb the memory. The missing margin comes from the <strong|cube>:
      at large fields it adds <math|<frac|1|4>*q<around|\||w|\|><rsup|4>>,
      and <math|1+<frac|1|4>-<frac|8|7>=<frac|3|28>\<gtr\>0>.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <with|font-size|1.6|font-series|bold|color|dark red|Forward coercivity>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture 5>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The scalar model>

    \;

    <\lemma*>
      <dueto|scalar forward coercivity>If
      <math|q*v<rsub|q>+<frac|\<gamma\>|3>*<around|(|v<rsub|q>+X<rsub|q>|)><rsup|3>=<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>v<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s+f<rsub|q>>
      on <math|<around|[|q<rsub|0>,T|]>>, then

      <\equation*>
        <frac|3|28>*<big|int>q<rsup|-1/2>*w<rsup|4>+<frac|\<gamma\>|16>*<big|int>q<rsup|-3/2>*w<rsup|6>\<le\><frac|4225|6>*\<gamma\>*<big|int>q<rsup|-3/2>*\<xi\><rsup|6>+<frac|12|\<gamma\>>*<big|int>q<rsup|-3/2>*f<rsup|2>,
      </equation*>

      and <math|<around|\||t<rsub|q>|\|>\<le\><frac|32|7>*<around|(|<around|\||w<rsub|q>|\|>+<around|\||\<xi\><rsub|q>|\|>|)>>.
      Pathwise, uniform in <math|\<gamma\>>.
    </lemma*>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The scalar model: a simulation>

    \;

    Forcing pulse <math|f<rsub|q>=\<kappa\>*<space|0.17em>q*r<rsub|q>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|[|a,2*a|]>><around|(|q|)>>,
    <math|\<kappa\>> of order one. Thresholds: <math|\<pm\><frac|3|4>> for
    <math|V<rsub|q>/r<rsub|q>>, <math|\<pm\>1> for
    <math|Y<rsub|q>/r<rsub|q>>; the Gaussian part <math|X<rsub|q>/r<rsub|q>>
    on its own panel.

    <image|<tuple|<#>|scalar_coercivity.pdf>|0.8par|||>

    The pulse pushes <math|V> past <math|<frac|3|4>*r>; the cube caps the
    excursion and brings it back. On this range
    <math|<around|\||X<rsub|q>|\|>\<gtr\>r<rsub|q>/32> most of the time:
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|q>> is not rare until <math|g<rsub|q>> is small (here
    <math|q\<gg\>10<rsup|6>>). In this realisation the Gaussian part stays
    below the threshold, <math|<around|\||X<rsub|q>|\|>/r<rsub|q>\<less\><frac|3|4>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Proof: test with <math|w<rsup|3>>>

    \;

    Where <math|w\<ne\>0>: <math|<around|\||v|\|>=<around|\||w|\|>+<frac|3|4>*r>,
    same sign.

    <\itemize>
      <item><with|font-series|bold|Mass:>
      <math|q*v*w<rsup|3>=q\|w\|<rsup|4>+<frac|3|4>*q*r<around|\||w|\|><rsup|3>>.

      <item><with|font-series|bold|Cube:>
      <math|<around|\||X|\|>\<le\><frac|r|32>+<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>>, so
      <math|<math-up|sgn><around|(|w|)>*<around|(|v+X|)>\<ge\><around|\||w|\|>+<frac|11|16>*r-<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>>;
      the cube is increasing, and

      <\equation*>
        <frac|\<gamma\>|3>*w<rsup|3>*<around|(|v+X|)><rsup|3>\<ge\><frac|\<gamma\>|12>*w<rsup|6>+<frac|q|4>*w<rsup|4>-<frac|4225|6>*\<gamma\>*\<xi\><rsup|6>*<space|2em><around|(|\<gamma\>*r<rsup|2>=q|)>.
      </equation*>

      <strong|Large-field coercivity: a sixth power and a quarter of the
      mass.>

      <item><with|font-series|bold|Memory:>
      <math|<around|\||v-w|\|>\<le\><frac|3|4>*r>,
      <math|<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>r\<le\><frac|2|3>*q*r>:
      <math|w<rsup|3>*<space|-0.17em><big|int>v\<le\><frac|1|2>*q*r\|w\|<rsup|3>+w<rsup|3>*<space|-0.17em><big|int>w>;
      the first is absorbed by <math|<frac|3|4>*q*r<around|\||w|\|><rsup|3>>.

      <item><with|font-series|bold|Forcing:>
      <math|w<rsup|3>*f\<le\><frac|\<gamma\>|48>*w<rsup|6>+<frac|12|\<gamma\>>*f<rsup|2>>.
    </itemize>

    Multiply by <math|q<rsup|-3/2>>, integrate, Hölder and Hardy:
    <math|<big|int>q<rsup|-3/2>*w<rsup|3>*<space|-0.17em><big|int>w\<le\><frac|8|7>*<big|int>q<rsup|-1/2>*w<rsup|4>>,
    and <math|<frac|5|4>-<frac|8|7>=<frac|3|28>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Two elementary inequalities>

    \;

    For <math|x,\<rho\>\<ge\>0>:<space|1em><math|<frac|1|4>*x<rsup|3>+64*\<rho\><rsup|3>-3*x<rsup|2>*\<rho\>=<frac|1|4>*<around|(|x-8*\<rho\>|)><rsup|2>*<around|(|x+4*\<rho\>|)>\<ge\>0>,
    hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|x-\<rho\>|)><rsup|3>\<ge\><tfrac|3|4>x<rsup|3>-65*\<rho\><rsup|3>.
    </equation*>

    With <math|x=u+<frac|11|16>*r>: <math|u<rsup|3>*x<rsup|3>\<ge\>u<rsup|6>+3<around|(|<tfrac|11|16>|)><rsup|2>*r<rsup|2>*u<rsup|4>>,
    <math|<frac|3|4><around|(|<frac|11|16>|)><rsup|2>=<frac|363|1024>\<gtr\><frac|1|4>>,
    and <math|<frac|65|3>*u<rsup|3>*\<rho\><rsup|3>\<le\><frac|1|6>*u<rsup|6>+<frac|4225|6>*\<rho\><rsup|6>>.
    So

    <\equation*>
      <frac|1|3>*u<rsup|3>*<around*|(|u+<frac|11|16>*r-\<rho\>|)><rsup|3>\<ge\><frac|1|12>*u<rsup|6>+<frac|1|4>*r<rsup|2>*u<rsup|4>-<frac|4225|6>*\<rho\><rsup|6>.
    </equation*>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|What the scalar lemma says>

      The excess of the forecast over the threshold is controlled by how
      often the <strong|Gaussian> field is large (known,
      <math|N>-independent) and by the <strong|backward> forcing. No
      smallness of the coupling; pathwise.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The lattice version>

    \;

    <\proposition*>
      <dueto|forward coercivity>For <math|q<rsub|0>> large, at every
      <math|N,T>, pathwise:<space|1em><math|J<rsub|T>\<le\>C*<around|(|G<rsub|T>+E<rsub|T>|)>>,
      <space|1em><math|<around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<le\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|3>*p<rsup|5>>.
    </proposition*>

    New terms:

    <\itemize>
      <item><with|font-series|bold|Laplacian:>
      <math|w<rsup|3>=\<phi\><rprime|'><around|(|V|)>>, <math|\<phi\>>
      convex, so <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<phi\><rprime|'><around|(|V|)>,-\<Delta\>*V|\<rangle\>>=N<rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|x\<sim\>x<rprime|'>><around|(|\<phi\><rprime|'><around|(|V<rsub|x>|)>-\<phi\><rprime|'><around|(|V<rsub|x<rprime|'>>|)>|)>*<around|(|V<rsub|x>-V<rsub|x<rprime|'>>|)>\<ge\>0>.

      <item><with|font-series|bold|Coordinate correction:>
      <math|\<varrho\>=\<varrho\><rsup|0>+\<varrho\><rsup|1>>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<varrho\><rsup|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<le\>\<varepsilon\>*r>
      (threshold shift; memory absorbed by the spare
      <math|<frac|1|4>*q*r<around|\||w|\|><rsup|3>>),
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<varrho\><rsup|1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j>\<le\>C*\<varepsilon\>*<around|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j>+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<xi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j>|)>>
      (memory absorbed by <math|<frac|3|28>>). Operator bounds:
      <math|\<varrho\>> is nonlocal but small.

      <item><with|font-series|bold|Backward forcing:>
      <math|\<gamma\><rsup|-1>*<big|int>q<rsup|-3/2><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsup|Z>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<le\>16*E<rsub|T>>.

      <item><with|font-series|bold|Gaussian tails:>
      <math|<math-up|Var>X<rsub|q><around|(|x|)>\<lesssim\><sqrt|q>> vs
      threshold <math|<frac|1|32>*<around|(|q/\<gamma\>|)><rsup|1/2>>:
      <math|<around*|\||<around|\<\|\|\>|\<xi\><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|j><rsup|j>|\|><rsub|p>\<le\>C<rsub|j>*p<rsup|j/2>*q<rsup|j/4>\<mathe\><rsup|-c<sqrt|q>/<around|(|p*\<gamma\>|)>>>.
    </itemize>

    <math|X> is Gaussian under the interacting measure: <strong|no
    information on the interacting field is used>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <with|font-size|1.6|font-series|bold|color|dark red|Backward feedback and
    closure>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture 5>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The backward representation>

    \;

    Propagator: <math|\<partial\><rsub|s>*<with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|q,s>=<with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|q,s>*H<rsub|s><around|(|Y<rsub|s>|)>>,
    <math|<with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|q,q>=I>, and by (A)

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|q,s>|\<\|\|\>>\<le\>L<rsub|0>*<space|0.17em><frac|s|q>,<space|2em>L<rsub|0>=exp
      <around*|(|<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|\<infty\>>\<varepsilon\><rsub|u>*<frac|\<mathd\>u|u>|)>\<le\>\<mathe\><rsup|C*<around|(|g<rsub|q<rsub|0>>+g<rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|2>|)>>.
    </equation*>

    <\lemma*>
      <dueto|conditional representation>

      <\equation*>
        Z<rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*<around*|[|<with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|q,T>*Z<rsub|T>+<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|T><with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|q,s>*<space|0.17em>b<rsub|s><around|(|Y<rsub|s>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s|]>.
      </equation*>
    </lemma*>

    Proof: <math|\<mathd\><rsub|s><around|(|<with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|q,s>*Z<rsub|s>|)>=-<with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|q,s>*b<rsub|s>\<mathd\>s+<with|math-font|cal*|P><rsub|q,s>*\<Xi\><rsub|s>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>>,
    a true martingale at fixed <math|N>.

    <math|Z> = expected source on the unresolved scales, transported back,
    plus the boundary error. Compare <math|F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|F<rsub|T>|]>>
    in Lecture 3: here the source is the <strong|defect>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Three contributions>

    \;

    <with|font-series|bold|Bounded fields> (two correction steps!):

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>>\<le\><big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>>L<rsub|0>*<frac|s|q>*<space|0.17em>C*\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*s<rsup|-5/2>\<mathd\>s=2*C*L<rsub|0>*\<gamma\><rsup|7/2>*q<rsup|-3/2>,<space|2em><tfrac|1|\<gamma\>><big|int>q<rsup|1/2><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsup|<math-up|b>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<le\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|6>*q<rsub|0><rsup|-3/2>.
    </equation*>

    <with|font-series|bold|Boundary:> <math|q<around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsup|<math-up|t>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>>\<le\>L<rsub|0>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*<around|\<\|\|\>|T*Z<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    contributing <math|<frac|2*L<rsub|0><rsup|2>|\<gamma\>*q<rsub|0><rsup|1/2>>*\<delta\><rsub|N,T,p>>,
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|N,T,p>=<around|\<\|\|\>|T*Z<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><rsub|L<rsup|2*p>*<around|(|\<bbb-P\>;L<rsup|2>|)>>>.

    <with|font-series|bold|Large fields:>
    <math|A<rsup|<math-up|l>>=<big|int>s<rsup|7/2><around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<le\>C*\<gamma\><rsup|3>*J<rsub|T>>;
    Cauchy\USchwarz and Jensen,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<le\>2*L<rsub|0><rsup|2>*q<rsup|-5/2>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*A<rsup|<math-up|l>>,<space|2em><around*|\||<tfrac|1|\<gamma\>><big|int>q<rsup|1/2><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsup|<math-up|l>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|\|><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<le\><frac|C*\<gamma\><rsup|2>|q<rsub|0>><around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>.
    </equation*>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|This is how the clipping error is controlled>

      <math|b<rsup|<math-up|l>>> is not small, but it enters the backward
      energy only through the large-field action <math|J<rsub|T>>, with the
      small factor <math|\<gamma\><rsup|2>/q<rsub|0>>.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Closing the loop>

    \;

    <\theorem*>
      <dueto|cutoff-uniform energy estimate>One
      <math|q<rsub|0>=q<rsub|0><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>>: for all <math|N>,
      <math|T>, <math|p\<ge\>1>,

      <\equation*>
        <around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<le\>C<rsub|\<gamma\>>*<around|(|1+p<rsup|5>+\<delta\><rsub|N,T,p>|)>.
      </equation*>
    </theorem*>

    <math|x=<around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>,
    <math|y=<around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>>:<space|1em><math|y\<le\>A<rsub|0>*<around|(|C*\<gamma\><rsup|3>*p<rsup|5>+x|)>>,
    <space|1em><math|x\<le\>B<rsub|0>+B<rsub|1>*\<delta\>+\<eta\>*y>,
    <space|1em><math|\<eta\>=C*\<gamma\><rsup|2>/q<rsub|0>=C*g<rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|2>>.
    Choose <math|A<rsub|0>*\<eta\>\<le\><frac|1|2>>;
    <math|y\<less\>\<infty\>> at fixed <math|N,T>, absorb.

    <with|par-mode|center|<\script-output|tikz|default>
      \\documentclass[tikz]{standalone}

      \\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}

      \\usepackage[sc]{mathpazo}

      \\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,mathtools,bm}

      \\usepackage[mathscr]{eucal}

      \\usetikzlibrary{decorations.pathreplacing,arrows.meta,positioning}

      \\usepackage[dvipsnames]{xcolor}

      \\definecolor{navy}{HTML}{1B2A41}

      \\definecolor{accent}{HTML}{B5402E}

      \\definecolor{SNavy}{HTML}{1B2A49}

      \\definecolor{STeal}{HTML}{1F7A72}

      \\definecolor{SGold}{HTML}{B8862B}

      \\begin{document}

      \\begin{tikzpicture}[

      \ \ box/.style={draw=navy,rounded corners=2pt,align=center,inner
      sep=4pt,font={}},

      \ \ arr/.style={-{Stealth[length=2.2mm]},thick,draw=navy!80}]

      \\node[box] (J) at (0,0) {large-field action $J_T$\\\\(forward,
      pathwise)};

      \\node[box] (E) at (7,0) {backward energy $E_T$\\\\(backward, in
      $L^p$)};

      \\draw[arr] ([yshift=0.2cm]E.west) -- node[above,font={}]{$J\\le
      C(G+E)$} ([yshift=0.2cm]J.east);

      \\draw[arr] ([yshift=-0.2cm]J.east) -- node[below,font={}]{$E\\le
      B+\\frac{C\\gamma^2}{q_0}J$} ([yshift=-0.2cm]E.west);

      \\end{tikzpicture}

      \\end{document}
    </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>|0.748par|||>>>

    The smallness is in the <strong|feedback>, independent of <math|p>: one
    <math|q<rsub|0>> for all <math|p>; the growth <math|p<rsup|5>> is
    inherited from <math|G<rsub|T>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Consequences>

    \;

    Once the boundary term is removed (Lecture 6):

    <\equation*>
      sup<rsub|N>\<\|\|\>E<rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>\<\|\|\><rsub|L<rsup|p>>+sup<rsub|N><around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<le\>C<rsub|\<gamma\>>*<around|(|1+p<rsup|5>|)>,<space|2em>sup<rsub|N>
      \<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsup|N><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<le\>C<rsub|\<gamma\>>*q<rsup|-5/2>,
    </equation*>

    and <math|\<bbb-E\>*exp <around|{|c*<around|(|E<rsub|\<infty\>>+J<rsub|\<infty\>>|)><rsup|1/5>|}>\<less\>\<infty\>>.

    <\itemize>
      <item>The approximate forecast differs from the exact one by
      <math|O<around|(|q<rsup|-5/4>|)>> in
      <math|L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-P\>;L<rsup|2>|)>> (root mean square),
      uniformly in the lattice.

      <item><math|H<rsub|q>*Z<rsub|q>=O<around|(|q<rsup|-9/4>|)>> in
      <math|L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-P\>;L<rsup|2>|)>>: up to an integrable
      drift, the forward equation is the explicit SDE
      <math|\<mathd\>Y=b<around|(|Y|)>\<mathd\>q+H<around|(|Y|)>\<mathd\>W>.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Comparison with the variational method>

    \;

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Comparison with Boué\UDupuis (Lecture 3)>

      There: drift <math|u=-4*\<lambda\>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2>:X<rsup|3>:+r>
      (<math|\<lambda\>*<big|int>:\<varphi\><rsup|4>>: of Lecture 3). Here:
      <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>> does all such changes of variables at
      once in the forecast coordinate; <math|Z> plays the role of <math|r>.
      Coercivity of the quartic: <strong|pathwise> in the forward equation;
      the remainder: <strong|in expectation> through the backward one.
    </myframed>

    <with|par-mode|right|<small|<with|color|dark grey|Barashkov\UG. 2020>>>
  </hidden>>
</body>

